WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:03.890
- Even outside of quantum computers,

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.530
some of the theories that
we've been talking about,

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.190
what's your hope,

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:11.336
what's most promising
to test these theories?

00:00:11.336 --> 00:00:14.360
What are kind of
experiments we can conduct,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.740
whether in simulation or
in the physical world,

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.360
that would validate or disprove
or expand these theories?

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.670
- Well I think for,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.010
there's two parts of that question.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:26.870
One is many-worlds

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.490
and the other one is sort
of emergent spacetime.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.050
For many-worlds, you know
there are experiments ongoing

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:35.280
to test whether or not wave
functions spontaneously collapse

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.860
and if they do then that
rules out many-worlds

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.480
and that will be falsified.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.650
What if there are hidden variables?

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.530
There's a theorem that seems to indicate

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.150
that the predictions will always
be the same as many-worlds.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.140
I'm a little skeptical of this
theorem, I'm not completely,

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.130
I haven't internalized it,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.450
I haven't made it in
part of my intuitive view

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.283
of the world yet,

00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:56.560
so there might be
loopholes to that theorem,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.880
I'm not sure about that.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:58.713
Part of me thinks that

00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:00.790
there should be different
experimental predictions

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:01.960
if there are hidden variables,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:02.903
but I'm not sure.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.530
But otherwise, it's just quantum
mechanics all the way down

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.820
and so there's this cottage
industry in science journalism

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.307
of writing breathless articles that say,

00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:16.030
"quantum mechanics shown
to be more astonishing

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.060
than ever before thought"

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.500
and really it's the same quantum mechanics

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.923
we've been doing since 1926.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.900
Whereas with the emergent spacetime stuff,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.420
we know a lot less about
what the theory is.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.600
It's in a very primitive state.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.180
We don't even really have
a safely written down,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.040
respectable, honest theory yet,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.400
so there could very well
be experimental predictions

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.650
we just don't know about yet.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.280
That is one of the things that
we're trying to figure out.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.340
- But for emergent spacetime,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.670
you need really big stuff, right?

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.980
- Well, or really fast stuff
or really energetic stuff,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.600
we don't know, that's the thing.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.630
You know, so there could be
violations of the speed of light

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.940
if you have emergent spacetime.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.860
Not going faster than the speed of light,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.490
but the speed of light could be different

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.500
for light of different wavelengths, right?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.340
That'll be a dramatic violation
of physics as we know it

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.010
but it could be possible.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.080
Or not, I mean it's not
an absolute prediction,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.080
that's the problem.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.803
The theories are just not well
developed enough yet to say.

